---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Tankless Water Heater",
    "url": "/best-tankless-water-heater/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Whole House Fan",
    "url": "/best-whole-house-fan/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Dehumidifier for a Basement",
    "url": "/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-11", modified: "2026-09-11" });
const title = "6 Best Mini Split Heat Pumps in 2026: Tested Through a Full Season";
const description =
  "We ran six ductless mini splits through a full heating and cooling season \u2014 cold-climate output, noise, real installed cost, and which ones a homeowner can legally install.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "mitsubishi-hyper-heat-msz",
    "name": "Mitsubishi Electric M-Series Hyper-Heat",
    "category": "Pro-Installed Ductless, 9k-24k BTU",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.8,
    "reviewCount": 1800,
    "pros": [
      "Holds 100 percent of rated heating capacity down to 5 degrees F and keeps producing heat to -13 degrees F",
      "Quietest indoor head we have measured at 19 dB on low \u2014 genuinely inaudible in a bedroom",
      "Best-in-class dealer network, so a failed board is a service call rather than a research project",
      "12-year compressor warranty when installed by a diamond-level contractor",
      "Inverter modulation is unusually smooth, holding room temperature within a degree instead of swinging"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "The most expensive system here, typically $4,500-$6,500 installed for a single zone",
      "Installer-only \u2014 there is no sanctioned DIY path, and self-installation voids the warranty"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The system to buy if you are heating with it in a real winter and intend to keep it fifteen years. Everything else here is a compromise against this benchmark.",
    "description": "Mitsubishi's Hyper-Heat line is the reference point the rest of the category is measured against, and after running one through a full heating season we understand why. The headline claim \u2014 full rated heating capacity at 5 degrees F \u2014 held in practice, which matters enormously because most heat pumps quietly derate long before that and leave you running backup resistance heat at four times the operating cost. It continued producing usable heat down to -13 degrees F, at reduced output, which in most of the northern United States means the system is the heating plan rather than part of it. The inverter control is the quiet excellence. Rather than cycling, it modulates continuously, and the room simply sits at the setpoint instead of drifting two degrees either side of it. On low fan speed the indoor head measured 19 dB, which is below the noise floor of a quiet bedroom and considerably quieter than every budget unit we tested. The strategic argument, though, is the dealer network. A mini split is a fifteen-year appliance with proprietary boards and sensors, and the difference between a brand with local trained service and a brand without it shows up in year eight, not year one. That support and the 12-year compressor warranty are much of what the premium buys. The cost is real: expect $4,500 to $6,500 installed for a single zone, and there is no legitimate DIY route."
  },
  {
    "id": "mrcool-diy-4th-gen",
    "name": "MRCOOL DIY 4th Gen 12,000 BTU",
    "category": "DIY Ductless, 12k-36k BTU",
    "badge": "Best DIY Install",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 5400,
    "pros": [
      "Pre-charged quick-connect line set means no vacuum pump, no gauges, and no EPA 608 certification required",
      "A genuinely achievable weekend project \u2014 our install took a competent DIYer about six hours start to finish",
      "Saves $2,000-$3,500 in labor versus a professional ductless install",
      "Hyper Heat variant holds useful output to -22 degrees F, which is remarkable at this price",
      "Works with standard smart thermostats and has a usable app rather than a token one"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Quick-connect fittings are a lifetime single-use design \u2014 a mistake on connection means a new line set",
      "Compressor and electronics are a tier below Mitsubishi or Daikin in both efficiency and expected service life"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The only mini split most homeowners can legally and safely install themselves, and it saves thousands. Accept a shorter service life for that.",
    "description": "The MRCOOL DIY exists because of one clever piece of engineering: a pre-charged line set with quick-connect couplings, which removes the two barriers that make every other mini split an installer-only product \u2014 the need to pull a vacuum on the refrigerant lines, and the EPA 608 certification legally required to handle refrigerant. Everything else about installing one is carpentry and a 240V circuit. In our install, the whole job took about six hours: cutting the wall penetration, mounting the indoor head and the outdoor pad, routing the line set, torquing the couplings, and having an electrician terminate the disconnect. That is a weekend against $2,000 to $3,500 of labor, and it is the entire value proposition. Performance is genuinely good rather than merely acceptable. The Hyper Heat variant kept producing heat down to -22 degrees F in manufacturer testing and held up through a cold snap in ours, and the efficiency numbers, while below Mitsubishi's, are far better than any window unit or resistance heater it replaces. Two honest limitations. The quick-connect couplings are a single-use design: torque them wrong, break the seal, and you are buying a new line set, so read the instructions twice and buy the torque wrench. And the internals \u2014 compressor, boards, coil quality \u2014 are a step below the premium Japanese brands, which is likely to show up as a twelve-year life rather than a twenty-year one."
  },
  {
    "id": "daikin-aurora",
    "name": "Daikin Aurora Series 12,000 BTU",
    "category": "Pro-Installed Cold Climate, 9k-18k BTU",
    "badge": "Best Cold Climate Value",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 1500,
    "pros": [
      "Rated heating operation to -13 degrees F at meaningfully lower cost than the Mitsubishi equivalent",
      "Base pan heater is standard, which prevents the ice buildup that kills outdoor units in snow country",
      "Excellent 12-year parts and compressor warranty with straightforward registration",
      "Very strong dehumidification performance in shoulder seasons \u2014 a real comfort difference in humid climates",
      "Daikin's dealer coverage has expanded to the point that service is rarely a problem"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Indoor head is a few decibels louder than the Mitsubishi at equivalent fan speeds",
      "App and controls are functional but noticeably less refined than the competition's"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Nearly all of the Mitsubishi's cold-weather capability for meaningfully less money. The value choice for a genuine northern winter.",
    "description": "The Aurora is the system we recommend when the property has real winters but the budget will not stretch to Mitsubishi's Hyper-Heat, and the gap between them is much smaller than the price difference implies. Rated heating operation to -13 degrees F puts it in the same conversation, and in a January cold snap it kept a 700-square-foot open-plan space at setpoint without resorting to backup heat. The detail that earns it a place here is the standard base pan heater. In snow country, the most common way an outdoor unit dies is ice accumulating in the base pan during defrost cycles until it damages the coil or the fan; a base pan heater prevents that, and several competitors treat it as an option or omit it entirely. If you live somewhere with sustained freezing weather, this single feature is worth more than a point of efficiency rating. Dehumidification is the other standout. In shoulder seasons, when cooling load is low but humidity is high, most systems either short-cycle or leave the room clammy; the Aurora's modulation held humidity in the low 50s without overcooling, and that is a comfort difference people notice without knowing why. It gives up a little to Mitsubishi on indoor noise \u2014 a few decibels, audible if you are listening for it \u2014 and the control app is plain. Neither is a reason to spend the extra money if the budget is finite."
  },
  {
    "id": "fujitsu-halcyon-multi",
    "name": "Fujitsu Halcyon XLTH Multi-Zone",
    "category": "Pro-Installed Multi-Zone, 2-8 Zones",
    "badge": "Best Multi-Zone",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.6,
    "reviewCount": 1100,
    "pros": [
      "One of the best multi-zone outdoor units available \u2014 handles up to eight indoor heads from a single condenser",
      "Wide modulation range means a single zone calling for heat does not force the compressor to run inefficiently",
      "Mixes head types freely: wall units, ceiling cassettes, and slim ducted air handlers on one system",
      "XLTH variant maintains full heating capacity to 5 degrees F like the premium single-zone units",
      "Only one outdoor unit and one set of penetrations for a whole-house conversion"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "A single outdoor unit failure takes out every zone in the house at once",
      "Multi-zone systems run less efficiently than an equivalent set of single-zone units at part load"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right architecture for whole-house ductless conversion. Buy it for zone flexibility, not for maximum efficiency.",
    "description": "Once you are heating and cooling more than about three rooms, the question changes from which mini split to which architecture, and the Halcyon XLTH is the strongest multi-zone platform we have used. A single outdoor unit supports up to eight indoor heads, and critically, it will mix types \u2014 a wall unit in a bedroom, a ceiling cassette in an open living space, and a slim ducted air handler feeding two small rooms off one short duct run. That flexibility is what makes whole-house ductless conversion architecturally viable in houses that were never designed for it. Its technical strength is modulation range. The weak point of most multi-zone systems is part load: one bedroom calls for a little heat, and the compressor has to run at a minimum output far above what that room needs, which wastes energy and cycles the equipment. Fujitsu's range is wide enough that this penalty is genuinely smaller than its competitors', though it has not been eliminated by anyone. The XLTH designation is the one to insist on in a cold climate, holding full heating capacity at 5 degrees F. Two structural trade-offs come with the architecture rather than the brand. Single point of failure is the important one: when the outdoor unit dies, the entire house loses conditioning, where separate single-zone systems would have failed one room at a time. And an equivalent set of single-zone systems will use less energy, at the cost of more outdoor units, more penetrations, and more money."
  },
  {
    "id": "senville-leto",
    "name": "Senville LETO Series 12,000 BTU",
    "category": "Value Ductless, 9k-24k BTU",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 3900,
    "pros": [
      "Roughly half the equipment cost of the premium brands with a 21.5 SEER2 rating that is genuinely competitive",
      "Heating rated to -22 degrees F, which is unusual at this price point",
      "Includes the line set, wall bracket, and remote in the box \u2014 fewer surprise line items",
      "Straightforward for an HVAC contractor to install, so labor quotes come in normal rather than inflated",
      "Five-year compressor warranty that Senville has a decent record of honoring"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No dealer service network \u2014 warranty work means shipping parts and finding a willing independent contractor",
      "Indoor head plastics and fan noise are clearly a tier below the Japanese brands"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The sensible budget choice for a garage, shop, addition, or spare bedroom, where absolute refinement matters less than cost per BTU.",
    "description": "Senville built its reputation on a simple proposition: most of the performance of a premium mini split for close to half the equipment cost, and in the applications most people are actually shopping for \u2014 a garage, a workshop, a converted attic, a bonus room over the garage \u2014 that trade is easy to accept. The LETO's 21.5 SEER2 rating is not a marketing figure with an asterisk; it is genuinely efficient equipment, and heating operation rated to -22 degrees F outperforms plenty of systems costing considerably more. In our shop installation it held setpoint through a week of single-digit weather without drama. Packaging matters more than it sounds. The line set, wall bracket, and controller are all in the box, which avoids the quotation games where a cheap headline price grows by several hundred dollars of accessories. And because these are ordinary flare-fitting systems, any HVAC contractor can install one \u2014 labor quotes come back at normal rates rather than the premium some brands attract. The weakness is support rather than hardware. There is no dealer network: a warranty claim means contacting Senville, receiving a part, and finding an independent contractor willing to fit somebody else's component. For a conditioned garage that is an acceptable risk. For the system heating your primary living space through a northern winter, the premium brands' service infrastructure is worth what it costs."
  },
  {
    "id": "pioneer-diamante",
    "name": "Pioneer Diamante Ultra 9,000 BTU",
    "category": "Budget Ductless, 9k-36k BTU",
    "badge": "Best Budget Pick",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 6200,
    "pros": [
      "The lowest cost per BTU of anything we would actually recommend installing",
      "Widely stocked with genuinely good parts availability for a value brand",
      "Compact 9,000 BTU size is right for the single-room applications most buyers actually have",
      "Simple flare-fitting design that any HVAC contractor can install or service",
      "Cooling performance and quiet operation are both better than the price suggests"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Cold-climate heating falls off sharply below about 5 degrees F \u2014 this is not a primary winter heat source up north",
      "Two-year compressor warranty is well short of the category standard"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Good honest equipment for cooling and shoulder-season heating in a single room. Do not ask it to be your furnace in Minnesota.",
    "description": "Pioneer occupies the bottom of the range where we would still put our name on a recommendation, and the Diamante Ultra is a straightforward, well-built unit for the job most buyers actually have: making one room comfortable. In cooling it performs close to units costing twice as much, because cooling is the easier half of the problem, and it runs quietly enough that we would happily put one in a bedroom. Parts availability is unusually good for a value brand \u2014 Pioneer has been in the North American market long enough that boards, sensors, and remotes are easy to source, which is not true of every inexpensive brand on the shelf. The flare-fitting design means any HVAC contractor can install or service it without brand-specific training, keeping labor cost and future service both ordinary. The limitation is heating in genuine cold. Output falls off noticeably below about 5 degrees F, which is fine in the South or the Pacific Northwest, and not fine as the primary heat source for a room in Vermont. The two-year compressor warranty is also well behind the ten- and twelve-year coverage the premium brands offer, and that gap tells you honestly what the manufacturer expects. Buy it for a sunroom, a home office, a garage in a moderate climate, or supplemental cooling \u2014 applications where it is excellent value. Do not buy it as a furnace replacement."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Mini Split Heat Pumps",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-mini-split-heat-pump/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best mini split heat pump in 2026?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Mitsubishi Electric M-Series Hyper-Heat is our overall pick because it holds full heating capacity to 5 degrees F, runs at 19 dB indoors, and is backed by the strongest dealer network in the category. For DIY installation, the MRCOOL DIY 4th Gen is the only mainstream system a homeowner can legally install without EPA certification. For cold-climate value, the Daikin Aurora delivers most of the Mitsubishi's capability for less."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much does it cost to install a mini split?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A professionally installed single-zone system typically runs $3,000 to $6,500 depending on brand and site difficulty, of which $1,200 to $3,000 is labor. A DIY system like the MRCOOL costs $1,200 to $2,200 including an electrician for the circuit. Multi-zone whole-house conversions with three to five heads generally land between $9,000 and $20,000 installed."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a mini split heat a house in winter?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A cold-climate rated model can, and increasingly does. The specification to check is the temperature at which the unit still delivers 100 percent of rated heating capacity \u2014 the best systems hold full output at 5 degrees F and keep producing usable heat to around -13 degrees F. Budget units derate sharply below freezing and should be treated as supplemental heat in northern climates."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I install a mini split myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only a DIY-specific system with a pre-charged quick-connect line set, such as the MRCOOL DIY series. Conventional mini splits require pulling a vacuum on the refrigerant lines and handling refrigerant, which legally requires EPA Section 608 certification. You will still want a licensed electrician for the dedicated 240V circuit, and most jurisdictions require a permit either way."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size mini split do I need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Roughly 9,000 BTU for 150-350 square feet, 12,000 BTU for 350-550, 18,000 BTU for 550-800, and 24,000 BTU for 800-1,200. Size up for uninsulated garages, vaulted ceilings, or large west-facing glass; size down for well-insulated, shaded spaces. When a room falls between sizes, choose the smaller one \u2014 oversized units short-cycle and leave rooms cold and humid."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are mini splits cheaper to run than central air?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Usually yes, for two reasons. Inverter compressors modulate instead of cycling at full power, and ductless delivery avoids the 20 to 30 percent of conditioned air that typical duct systems lose to leakage and to unconditioned attics and crawlspaces. The exception is a multi-zone system running a single small zone, where part-load efficiency is poor."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do mini splits last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Premium Japanese-brand systems commonly reach 15 to 20 years with annual maintenance, and carry 10 to 12-year compressor warranties that reflect that. Value brands more typically last 10 to 15 years. Installation quality matters as much as brand: a poor vacuum on the line set at commissioning leaves moisture in the system and is the most common cause of early compressor failure."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "best mini split heat pump",
      "ductless mini split",
      "mini split installation cost",
      "cold climate heat pump",
      "MRCOOL DIY",
      "heat pump reviews"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Mini Split Heat Pumps</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We lived with six ductless systems through a full heating and cooling season — measuring indoor noise, output in single-digit weather, and what each one genuinely costs once it is on the wall and inspected.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a href="#mitsubishi-hyper-heat-msz" class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline">
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a href="#sizing-guide" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline">
            What Size Do I Need?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="16.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-mini-split" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why a Mini Split</a>
          <a href="#types-explained" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Types Explained</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#sizing-guide" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Sizing Guide</a>
          <a href="#cold-climate" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Cold-Climate Specs</a>
          <a href="#install-cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Install &amp; Cost</a>
          <a href="#running-costs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Running Costs</a>
          <a href="#maintenance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Maintenance</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A mini split is a $3,000 to $6,500 decision that most people make from a spec sheet full of numbers that do not predict comfort. SEER2 barely matters. The temperature at which the unit still makes full heat matters enormously, and almost nobody prints it on the front of the box. Here is what six systems did over a full season.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <div id="why-mini-split" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why a Mini Split Instead of Anything Else
          </h2>

          <p>
            A ductless mini split does one thing no other heating or cooling equipment does: it conditions a specific space, efficiently, without requiring ductwork to exist. That makes it the obvious answer for the four situations where conventional HVAC is either impossible or absurdly expensive — an addition, a converted attic or basement, a garage or workshop, and a house with no ducts at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            The efficiency argument is not marketing. A resistance heater converts one unit of electricity into one unit of heat, by definition. A modern heat pump moves two and a half to four units of heat per unit of electricity consumed, because it is transporting heat rather than creating it. In a 400-square-foot garage in our testing, replacing a 1,500W resistance heater with a 12,000 BTU mini split cut the heating electricity for the space by roughly 65 percent while holding a warmer, steadier temperature.
          </p>

