          <p>
            The second advantage is zoning that actually works. Central systems condition the whole house to satisfy one thermostat, which is why the bedroom over the garage is always wrong. A mini split gives that room its own setpoint and its own compressor modulation, and the comfort difference in problem rooms is larger than any duct rebalancing will achieve.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest disadvantages are two. The indoor head is visible on the wall, and no amount of industrial design makes it furniture — some people never stop noticing it. And a mini split conditions the space it is in; it will not push warm air around a corner into a closed bedroom, which means a house needs one head per genuinely separated space rather than one system per floor.
          </p>
