        <!-- SECTION 5 -->
        <div id="cold-climate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cold-Climate Performance and the Specs That Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>The rated-capacity temperature is the spec that matters most in the north, and it is the one manufacturers bury.</strong> Every heat pump produces less heat as it gets colder. What separates a cold-climate system is how far down it holds full capacity. A unit that delivers 100 percent of rated output at 5 degrees F is a heating system; one that derates to 60 percent at 20 degrees F is a supplement that will leave you running expensive backup heat all winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>HSPF2</strong> measures heating efficiency across a season, and <strong>SEER2</strong> measures cooling efficiency. Both matter, but the gap between a good unit and an excellent one is smaller in dollars than buyers assume: moving from 18 to 22 SEER2 on a single zone typically saves $40 to $90 a year depending on climate and electricity rate. Do not pay a thousand dollars for four points of SEER2 on a garage.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Defrost strategy</strong> is the underrated spec. In humid cold weather the outdoor coil frosts, and the system must periodically reverse to melt it, during which it is briefly blowing cool air indoors. Better systems defrost on demand using coil sensors rather than on a fixed timer, which means fewer interruptions and less wasted energy. Ask specifically; it is rarely on the brochure.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Base pan heaters and mounting height</strong> decide whether the outdoor unit survives snow country. Melt water refreezing in the base pan is a common failure, and an outdoor unit mounted at grade in a region that gets three feet of snow will be buried. Wall brackets or a tall stand plus a base pan heater are not accessories in the north; they are the install.
          </p>
        </div>

