        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <div id="running-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What It Costs to Run
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 12,000 BTU mini split draws roughly 700 to 1,100 watts while actively cooling and rather less once the space is at temperature and the inverter has throttled back. At 16 cents per kilowatt-hour, cooling a 400-square-foot room through a hot summer month costs in the region of $25 to $40 — meaningfully less than a window unit doing the same job, because the window unit cycles on and off at full draw instead of modulating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heating is where the arithmetic gets interesting, and where the comparison depends entirely on what you are replacing. Against electric resistance heat, a mini split costs roughly a third as much to run, and the payback on the equipment is often two to four winters. Against natural gas at typical prices, the running cost is broadly comparable, and the case for the mini split is cooling, zoning, and not having a flue — not fuel savings. Against propane or heating oil, the heat pump usually wins clearly.
          </p>

          <p>
            One real-world factor that spreadsheets miss: because a mini split is cheap to run and quiet, people use it. Rooms that were previously heated to 60 degrees and tolerated end up at 68 degrees and comfortable, and the bill does not fall as far as the efficiency math predicted. That is not a failure of the equipment; it is comfort you were previously going without, and most owners consider it the point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Maintenance That Actually Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mini splits ask for very little, but the little they ask for is not optional, and neglect shows up as a smell before it shows up as a fault.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wash the indoor filters every month during heavy use — they pull out and rinse under a tap in two minutes, and a clogged filter costs efficiency immediately. Once a year, clean the blower wheel and evaporator coil or have it done; the wheel accumulates a biofilm that is the source of the musty smell people associate with mini splits, and it is the single most common complaint about otherwise healthy systems. Keep the condensate drain clear, because a blocked drain overflows onto the wall below the head.
          </p>

          <p>
            Outside, keep the coil clear of leaves and grass clippings, keep at least a foot of clearance on all sides, and in snow country make sure the unit is mounted high enough to stay above drift line. Once every couple of years, a technician should verify refrigerant charge and check the electrical connections — a slow leak degrades performance gradually enough that owners adapt to it without noticing until the system stops heating properly in January.
          </p>
        </div>


