        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mini Split Buying Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mini splits are reliable equipment that fails most often for reasons chosen at the quoting stage rather than built into the hardware.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Oversizing the Unit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                It is the most common error and it produces exactly the wrong result: a room that cools quickly, shuts off, never dehumidifies, and feels clammy, while the compressor cycles far more than it was designed to. Size to the room, and when in doubt on a well-insulated space, go smaller.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing on SEER2 Instead of Cold-Climate Rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Efficiency ratings are measured in mild conditions. If you are heating with the unit in a real winter, the temperature at which it still delivers full rated capacity matters far more than four points of SEER2 — and a high-SEER2 unit that derates at 20 degrees F will cost you more to run than a slightly less efficient cold-climate model.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Vacuum, or Hiring Someone Who Does</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Evacuating the line set to remove air and moisture before releasing the charge is the step that determines compressor life, and it is invisible on the finished job. Ask any installer what micron level they pull down to and how long they hold it. If the answer is vague, keep calling.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mounting the Outdoor Unit at Grade in Snow Country</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A unit sitting on a pad at ground level will be buried by drifting snow and will sit in its own defrost melt water. In any region with sustained snow, mount it on wall brackets or a tall stand, well above the typical drift line, and insist on a base pan heater.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Annual Coil and Blower Cleaning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The musty smell people associate with mini splits is biofilm on the blower wheel and evaporator coil, and it is entirely preventable. One cleaning a year keeps the system smelling neutral and running at rated efficiency; three years of neglect requires a deep clean that costs several hundred dollars.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

